        "Symmetric ciphers use one shared key for both directions. AES, the current standard, encrypts 128-bit blocks with keys of 128, 192 or 256 bits through rounds of substitution and permutation, and it is hardware-accelerated in every modern CPU. It encrypts quickly, so bulk data, disk volumes and VPN tunnels all ride on AES. The permanent problem is key distribution: both parties need the same secret safely, which asymmetric cryptography then solves, as {S} handshakes show.",
