        "Nigeria's Cybercrimes (Prohibition, Prevention, etc.) Act 2015 is the core statute: it criminalises unauthorised access, illegal interception, data interference, system interference, identity theft, cyberstalking and attacks on critical information infrastructure, and it frames lawful interception under judicial oversight. Financial institutions carry specific duties under it. Knowing which provision covers which conduct matters for {S} professionals who must comply, report and testify.",
        "Data protection began with the NDPR of 2019 under NITDA and matured into the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023, which established the commission and a data protection commissioner. It sets lawful bases for processing, data subject rights, breach notification, cross-border transfer rules and penalties with teeth. Organisations must appoint responsible officers where scale demands and keep processing records. For {S}, the Act converts privacy from courtesy into obligation.",
        "Regulation splits by technology. NITDA develops IT policy, issues guidelines for government systems and drove the NDPR; the NCC licenses telecommunications operators, manages spectrum and sets service benchmarks; the CBN supervises payment security in banking; ngCERT coordinates incident response. Knowing which regulator owns which question saves professionals from writing to the wrong body and shapes compliance calendars across {S} practice.",
        "ngCERT is Nigeria's national computer emergency response team, created under the Cybercrimes Act framework. It issues advisories and bulletins, coordinates responses to major incidents, operates reporting channels for citizens and organisations, and runs capacity building. Sectoral teams feed it. For organisations, the practical duties are knowing how to report, preserving evidence and subscribing to advisories so patches and threats are known early, the routine {S} organisations build.",
        "Nigeria anchors identity in the Bank Verification Number for banking and the National Identification Number for civil identity, both biometric databases holding tens of millions of records. They reduce fraud and enable credit, but concentrate risk: a breach would be catastrophic, so the NDPA treats such data as high-risk, demanding strict access control, encryption and audit trails. Handling BVN or NIN data is among the most sensitive stewardship duties in {S}.",
