        "Digital forensics turns systems into evidence. Principles are strict: acquire without altering originals, image disks with verified hashes, maintain chain of custody, analyse copies and document every step so courts can trust the findings. Tools write-block and hash simultaneously. Logs, disk images, phone extractions and network captures all qualify when handled properly, and sloppy handling, such as booting a suspect machine casually, can render evidence inadmissible, the failure {S} warns against.",
        "Ethics guide power. Professionals access systems only under authority, protect confidentiality learned at work, disclose responsibly and avoid conflicts of interest. Codes from bodies such as ISACA, ISC2 and Nigeria's Computer Professionals Registration Council set standards, and employment or professional registration can hinge on them. Hacking without written authorisation remains a crime regardless of intent, a line the best technical students must respect, as {S} codes state.",
        "Penetration testing simulates attacks to find weaknesses first, but its legality depends entirely on written authorisation: scope, systems, methods, timing and rules of engagement, signed before work begins. Testing outside scope is unlawful even with good intent. Deliverables classify findings by risk with remediation advice, and retests verify fixes. The discipline exists because authorised attack is the only reliable way to measure defence, the rationale {S} gives for authorisation.",
